        <div id="voltage-connectors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Voc, Voltage Windows and Connectors
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important number on a solar panel's label is not its wattage. It is Voc, the open-circuit voltage, which is the highest voltage the panel produces when nothing is drawing current. Every power station's solar input has a voltage window, commonly 11 or 12 to 60 volts on portable models. Stay under the maximum, including on the coldest morning you expect, and you are safe. Go over, and the station may refuse to charge or be damaged.
          </p>

          <p>
            Voc rises as temperature falls, by around 0.3 percent per degree Celsius for most silicon cells. A panel rated at 48V at 25°C can read over 52V at freezing. That is why we keep about 10 to 15 percent headroom below the station's maximum. The second number, Vmp or working voltage, matters at the other end: it must stay above the station's minimum for charging to start, which can be an issue with a single low-voltage panel on a hazy day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Connectors are the easier problem, because adapters exist for almost every combination. MC4 is the industry standard used by EcoFlow, Anker, Bluetti and Renogy panels. Stations use XT60 (EcoFlow and Anker), DC7909 (older Jackery and many Bluetti), DC8020 (current Jackery), or Anderson Powerpole and 8mm plugs (Goal Zero). An adapter fixes a physical mismatch; it does nothing about a voltage mismatch, so always check Voc first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the adapter from the station's manufacturer or a reputable brand, and check its current rating. Cheap adapters with thin wire can run warm at 10 amps or more, which is common with 200W and larger panels. Keep connections off wet ground, and avoid running long, thin extension cables, which waste power as heat. A 10 to 12 gauge cable is appropriate for runs over about 15 feet.
          </p>

