        <div id="series-parallel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Series vs Parallel: Combining Panels
          </h2>

          <p>
            When one panel is not enough, you connect two or more. Wiring in series connects positive of one panel to negative of the next; voltages add while current stays the same. Wiring in parallel connects positive to positive and negative to negative, usually with an MC4 branch connector; currents add while voltage stays the same. Both give the same total watts on paper. The difference is how well they fit your station's input.
          </p>

          <p>
            Series is usually better on portable stations. Two EcoFlow 220W bifacial panels in series reach about 44V open-circuit and deliver their power at around 37V, which keeps current low and within most inputs' amp limits. Higher voltage also loses less in long cables and helps the station start charging earlier in the morning. The drawback is shade: because the current is shared, shading one panel drags down the whole string.
          </p>

          <p>
            Parallel keeps voltage low and adds current. Two panels at 20V and 11A each become 20V and 22A, which exceeds the 15A limit on many stations, so the station clips the output. Parallel is the right choice when series would exceed the voltage limit, such as two Anker PS100s at 57V open-circuit on a 60V station, or when one panel is often in partial shade and you do not want it dragging down the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only combine panels with similar electrical specifications. Wiring a 100W panel in series with a 200W panel limits the pair to the smaller panel's current, wasting much of the larger panel's output. If you mix, parallel is less wasteful, but identical panels are best. And never exceed the station's maximum voltage in series. Add each panel's Voc together, add 10 to 15 percent for cold weather, and compare with the input limit.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Series for amps, parallel for volts.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Series wiring helps when a station's amp limit clips output. Parallel wiring helps when series would exceed the voltage limit. Check both numbers against the input, with cold-weather headroom.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cell-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Monocrystalline, Bifacial and N-Type Cells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every panel on our list uses monocrystalline silicon cells, and that is now the default for portable panels. Monocrystalline cells are more efficient than the older polycrystalline and thin-film types, so a panel of the same wattage is smaller and lighter. For a product you fold up and carry, that matters more than a small price difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bifacial panels have cells that collect light from both sides. The front works like any panel; the back picks up light reflected from the ground. The gain depends almost entirely on the surface below. Over snow, white gravel, sand, concrete or a pale tarp, the back face can add a meaningful amount of energy. Over dark grass or soil it adds little. Manufacturer claims of up to 25 percent more energy are best cases.
          </p>

          <p>
            To get the most from a bifacial panel, raise it off the ground so light can reach the rear, and place it over the palest surface available. A light-coloured tarp under the panel is a cheap way to add output. A bifacial panel lying flat on a lawn performs like a normal panel of the same front rating, so do not pay much extra unless you can use the rear face.
          </p>

          <p>
            N-type cells, used in the Renogy suitcase and increasingly in other panels, degrade more slowly and cope better with heat than older P-type cells. Renogy quotes under 0.4 percent annual degradation. For a panel used occasionally for camping, it makes little difference. For a panel used daily for years, it adds up to more energy over the panel's life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="portability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Weight, Fold Size and Kickstands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable panels range from about 9 pounds for a 100W folding panel to over 40 pounds for a 400W panel in its case. Weight scales roughly with wattage, and there is no way around it. Decide how far you will carry the panel. From a car to a campsite, a 200W panel at 15 to 20 pounds is manageable. For a walk of more than a few minutes, 100W panels are much easier, and two of them can be carried separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unfolded size matters as much as weight. A 400W panel can be close to 8 feet long, which needs a large open space and can be hard to position in a crowded campsite. Folded size determines whether it fits in a car boot or behind a seat. Check both dimensions on the listing, and measure the space you have before buying the largest panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kickstands decide how much of the rated output you actually get. A panel angled toward the sun captures far more than one lying flat, especially in spring, autumn and winter, when the sun is low. The Anker PS100 locks at four angles, the most adjustable here. Most other panels use a single angle or a case that folds into a stand. A rough rule is to tilt the panel at an angle close to your latitude, steeper in winter and flatter in summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wind is the other practical concern. A large panel is a sail, and a gust can flip it, crack cells or pull the connectors out. Use guy lines, weigh the kickstand down, or lay the panel on a slope rather than propping it high on a windy day. Check the panel's corner eyelets for tie-down points before you need them.
          </p>

