        <div id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Weatherproofing: IP65, IP67 and IP68
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable panels live outdoors, so weather protection matters. The IP rating gives two digits: the first for dust, the second for water. All the panels here are dust-tight (6). The difference is water. IP65 resists water jets and rain but is not designed for immersion. IP67 survives temporary immersion. IP68 is rated for longer or deeper immersion, as defined by the manufacturer.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practical terms, an IP65 panel like the Bluetti PV350 should come in during heavy, prolonged rain, and its connectors should never sit in a puddle. An IP67 or IP68 panel, such as the Anker PS100 or EcoFlow 220W, handles a surprise storm without worry. For panels left out for days at a cabin or during a long outage, IP67 or better is worth prioritising.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rating applies to the panel and the sealed junction box, not necessarily to the cables and adapters. Built-in USB ports, open connectors and adapters are the weak points. Keep MC4 connections off the ground and pointing downward, cap unused USB ports, and never leave an adapter lying in wet grass. The station itself is not weatherproof and should always stay dry and shaded.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface material also affects durability. ETFE, used on the Jackery SolarSaga panels, resists scratches, UV and yellowing better than the PET laminate on cheaper panels. Rigid glass panels like the Renogy suitcase are the most durable surface but are heavier and can crack if dropped. Fabric-backed folding panels are lightest but should be kept away from sharp edges and not folded while wet for long periods.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Matching Panels to Your Power Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right panel depends on the station it will feed. Start with the station's maximum solar input in watts, its voltage window and its amp limit. Buy enough panel wattage to reach the maximum input in real-world conditions, which usually means rated panel watts of about 1.2 to 1.4 times the input limit if you want to reach it in less-than-perfect sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            For small stations under 500Wh, a single 100W or 200W panel is plenty. For 1kWh stations with 400W to 600W inputs, two 200W panels in series are the sweet spot. For 2kWh stations with 1,000W or more of input, a pair of 400W panels or several 200W panels makes sense. Our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">best solar generator</a> guide shows each kit's input limits side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be realistic about what the panels will run. A refrigerator uses 1 to 2kWh per day, which means roughly 400 to 600W of panels in good sun just to keep up. Our guide on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">a portable power station can run a refrigerator</a> covers the startup surge and runtime maths. A CPAP, a router and lights are far easier to cover with one or two panels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about future growth. Buying two identical panels now makes series or parallel wiring simple later. Buying one high-voltage panel may make it harder to add a second if your station has a 60V limit. If you might upgrade to a larger station with a high-voltage input, such as the stations in our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power station</a> roundup, panels with standard MC4 connectors and moderate voltages will move with you.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">PANEL WATTS FOR COMMON STATIONS</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100-200W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Stations under 500Wh</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">400W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1kWh stations, one sunny day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">800W+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">2kWh stations for same-day refills</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.2-1.4x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Panel watts vs input limit</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
