        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the rated wattage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A panel's rating comes from lab conditions that rarely occur outdoors. Expect 60 to 80 percent of the rated watts at a sunny midday and much less in the morning, evening or under cloud. Plan your daily energy using 70 percent of the rating and realistic peak sun hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring open-circuit voltage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Checking only the connector is how controllers get damaged. Add up the Voc of panels in series, add 10 to 15 percent for cold mornings, and compare with the station's maximum input voltage. An adapter solves a plug mismatch, not a voltage problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing different panels in series</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Combining a 100W and a 200W panel in series limits both to the smaller panel's current and wastes much of the larger panel's output. Use identical panels where possible, and if you must mix, wire them in parallel within the station's amp limit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying panels flat in shade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A flat panel in partial shade can lose half its output or more. Prop the panel toward the sun, use the kickstand at a steeper angle in winter, and move it as shadows shift. Full sun at a slightly wrong angle beats partial shade every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving an IP65 panel out in heavy rain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                IP65 resists splashes and rain but is not designed for immersion, and connectors are weak points on any panel. Bring lower-rated panels in during storms, keep connections off wet ground, and never let the power station itself get wet.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable solar panel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the EcoFlow 220W Bifacial is the best all-rounder: high output, a 21.8V Voc that pairs safely in series, IP68 weather protection and a standard MC4 connector. The Jackery SolarSaga 100W is best when weight matters, and the EcoFlow 400W is best for refilling large stations quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power does a 100W portable solar panel produce?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 60 to 80 watts at a sunny midday, or about 350Wh on a day with five peak sun hours. That keeps a small station topped up and charges phones and lights easily, but it takes about three good days to refill a 1kWh power station from empty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a different brand of solar panel with my power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, if the panel's voltage and current fit within the station's input window and you have the right adapter. Most panels use MC4 connectors, while stations use XT60, DC7909, DC8020 or Anderson. Check Voc with cold-weather headroom before connecting any third-party panel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I wire solar panels in series or parallel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Series is usually better on portable stations, because it raises voltage and keeps current within amp limits, and it loses less in long cables. Choose parallel if series would exceed the station's voltage limit, or if one panel is often shaded. Use identical panels either way.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are bifacial solar panels worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you can use the rear face. Over snow, sand, concrete or a light tarp, a bifacial panel can add useful output. Over dark grass it performs like a normal panel. Raise it off the ground so reflected light reaches the back, and do not pay a large premium if you cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can portable solar panels get wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most can handle rain. IP67 and IP68 panels resist immersion, while IP65 panels handle splashes and light rain but should be brought in during heavy storms. Cables, adapters and USB ports are weak points on any panel, and the power station itself must always stay dry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many portable solar panels do I need to run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A refrigerator uses about 1 to 2kWh per day, so you need roughly 400 to 600W of panels in good summer sun just to keep up, plus a station of 1 to 2kWh to cover the night. In winter or cloudy weather, plan on more panels or a backup charging source.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

